**Claude (claude.ai and the desktop app):** Settings, then Connectors, then Add custom connector, and paste the URL.

**Claude Code:**

```bash
claude mcp add --transport http fabrica https://mcp.fabrica.land/mcp
claude mcp add --transport http fabrica-testnet https://mcp-testnet.fabrica.land/mcp
```

**Cursor** (`.cursor/mcp.json`):

```json
{
  "mcpServers": {
    "fabrica": { "url": "https://mcp.fabrica.land/mcp" }
  }
}
```

Any other client that supports remote MCP servers over Streamable HTTP is configured the same way.

```
GET https://api.fabrica.land/valuation/v1/{networkName}/{contractAddress}/{tokenId}
```

The oracle signs each quote as an EIP-712 typed structure using the following type hash:

```solidity
bytes32 constant QUOTE_TYPEHASH = keccak256(
    "Quote(address token,uint256 tokenId,address currency,uint256 price,uint64 timestamp,uint64 duration)"
);
```

```typescript
import { recoverTypedDataAddress } from 'viem'

const valuationPanel = await fetch(
  `https://api.fabrica.land/valuation/v1/ethereum/${tokenContract}/${tokenId}`
).then((r) => r.json())

// Select the entry whose source/domain matches the oracle used by your pool.
const sourceValuation = valuationPanel.valuations.find(
  (entry) => entry.source === 'All Fabrica Properties'
)

if (!sourceValuation?.quote || !sourceValuation.signature) {
  throw new Error('No signed valuation available for this source')
}

const recoveredSigner = await recoverTypedDataAddress({
  domain: {
    name: 'All Fabrica Properties', // read from oracle's eip712Domain() in production
    version: '1.2',
    chainId: 1,
    verifyingContract: '0xA23887308d75154C50Cbb7F8bbD5DC9EA40c38Ac', // Ethereum mainnet
  },
  types: {
    Quote: [
      { name: 'token', type: 'address' },
      { name: 'tokenId', type: 'uint256' },
      { name: 'currency', type: 'address' },
      { name: 'price', type: 'uint256' },
      { name: 'timestamp', type: 'uint64' },
      { name: 'duration', type: 'uint64' },
    ],
  },
  primaryType: 'Quote',
  message: {
    token: sourceValuation.quote.token,
    tokenId: BigInt(sourceValuation.quote.tokenId),
    currency: sourceValuation.quote.currency,
    price: BigInt(sourceValuation.quote.price),
    timestamp: BigInt(sourceValuation.quote.timestamp),
    duration: BigInt(sourceValuation.quote.duration),
  },
  signature: sourceValuation.signature,
})

// Compare against the registered signer
const expectedSigner = await publicClient.readContract({
  address: priceOracleAddress,
  abi: priceOracleAbi,
  functionName: 'priceOracleSigner',
  args: [tokenContract],
})

if (
  recoveredSigner.toLowerCase() !== sourceValuation.signer.toLowerCase() ||
  recoveredSigner.toLowerCase() !== expectedSigner.toLowerCase()
) {
  throw new Error('Invalid signer')
}
```

The onchain verification path is implemented in `SimpleSignedPriceOracle._verifyQuote`. To verify in your own contract, hash the typed data per EIP-712 and recover with `ECDSA.recover`:

```solidity
import {ECDSA} from "@openzeppelin/contracts/utils/cryptography/ECDSA.sol";
import {EIP712} from "@openzeppelin/contracts/utils/cryptography/EIP712.sol";

bytes32 constant QUOTE_TYPEHASH = keccak256(
    "Quote(address token,uint256 tokenId,address currency,uint256 price,uint64 timestamp,uint64 duration)"
);

function verifyQuote(
    address token,
    uint256 tokenId,
    address currency,
    uint256 price,
    uint64 timestamp,
    uint64 duration,
    bytes calldata signature,
    address expectedSigner
) external view {
    bytes32 structHash = keccak256(abi.encode(
        QUOTE_TYPEHASH,
        token,
        tokenId,
        currency,
        price,
        timestamp,
        duration
    ));

    address recovered = ECDSA.recover(_hashTypedDataV4(structHash), signature);
    require(recovered == expectedSigner, "Invalid signer");
    require(timestamp <= block.timestamp, "Quote not yet valid");
    require(timestamp + duration >= block.timestamp, "Quote expired");
    require(price > 0, "Zero price");
}
```

***

The reference onchain consumer is the Fabrica `SimpleSignedPriceOracle` (a fork of MetaStreet's audited contract). It conforms to the generic `IPriceOracle` (https://github.com/fabrica-land/fabrica-v3-contracts/blob/main/src/interfaces/IPriceOracle.sol) interface, which any pool can implement against:

```solidity
interface IPriceOracle {
    function price(
        address collateralToken,
        address currencyToken,
        uint256[] memory tokenIds,
        uint256[] memory tokenIdQuantities,
        bytes calldata oracleContext
    ) external view returns (uint256 price);
}
```

The pool calls `priceOracle.price(...)` during loan origination, passing an `oracleContext` that the consumer pool obtained from the borrower (typically supplied alongside `borrow(...)` calldata).

For `SimpleSignedPriceOracle`, `oracleContext` is the ABI-encoded array of `SignedQuote` structs, one per `tokenId` in the same order as `tokenIds`:

```solidity
struct Quote {
    address token;
    uint256 tokenId;
    address currency;
    uint256 price;
    uint64 timestamp;
    uint64 duration;
}

struct SignedQuote {
    Quote quote;
    bytes signature;
}

// oracleContext = abi.encode(SignedQuote[])
```

In TypeScript, with the `@metastreet/sdk-v2` `QuoteHelper`, encode the signed `sourceValuation` entry selected from the panel:

```typescript
import { QuoteHelper } from '@metastreet/sdk-v2'
import { encodePacked, fromHex } from 'viem'

const signedQuote = {
  quote: {
    token: sourceValuation.quote.token,
    tokenId: BigInt(sourceValuation.quote.tokenId),
    currency: sourceValuation.quote.currency,
    price: BigInt(sourceValuation.quote.price),
    timestamp: BigInt(sourceValuation.quote.timestamp),
    duration: BigInt(sourceValuation.quote.duration),
  },
  signature: sourceValuation.signature,
}

const encodedQuote = QuoteHelper.encodeQuotes([signedQuote])

// Wrap in the pool's borrow-options envelope (tag 5 = oracle context)
const numBytes = fromHex(encodedQuote, 'bytes').length
const options = encodePacked(['uint16', 'uint16', 'bytes'], [5, numBytes, encodedQuote])
```

The `options` bytes are then passed as the final argument to the pool's `borrow(...)` function.

